{"task_id": "HumanEval/158", "solution": "def find_max(words):\n    \"\"\"Returns the word with the maximum number of unique characters.\n    If multiple words tie, the lexicographically smallest is returned.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not words:\n        return \"\"\n\n    # Initialize with the first word in the list.\n    # This ensures `result_word` and `max_unique_chars` always have valid values\n    # if the input list is not empty.\n    result_word = words[0]\n    max_unique_chars = len(set(words[0]))\n\n    # Iterate through the rest of the words starting from the second element.\n    for word in words[1:]:\n        current_unique_chars = len(set(word))\n\n        if current_unique_chars > max_unique_chars:\n            # Found a word with more unique characters, update the maximum.\n            max_unique_chars = current_unique_chars\n            result_word = word\n        elif current_unique_chars == max_unique_chars:\n            # If unique character count is the same, apply lexicographical tie-breaking.\n            if word < result_word:\n                result_word = word\n    \n    return result_word"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/158", "solution": "def find_max(words: list[str]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Returns the word with the maximum number of unique characters.\n    If multiple words have the same maximum, the lexicographically smallest is returned.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not words:\n        return \"\"\n\n    # Sort the words lexicographically first to handle tie-breaking implicitly.\n    # When unique character counts are equal, the first word encountered (which is\n    # the lexicographically smallest due to sorting) will be chosen.\n    sorted_words = sorted(words)\n\n    max_unique_chars = -1\n    result_word = \"\"\n\n    for word in sorted_words:\n        current_unique_chars = len(set(word))\n        \n        if current_unique_chars > max_unique_chars:\n            max_unique_chars = current_unique_chars\n            result_word = word\n            \n    return result_word"}
